[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"UObject Reflection System and Garbage Collection Mechanics in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unreal Engine's architecture heavily relies on two foundational pillars: the Reflection System and the Garbage Collection (GC) mechanism. Understanding how these systems work under the hood is crucial for writing performant, memory-safe C++ code in Unreal Engine."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The UObject Reflection System"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The reflection system (or Type Introspection) allows Unreal Engine to examine metadata about classes, functions, and properties at runtime. This powers features like Blueprints, network replication, serialization, the Editor Details panel, and garbage collection."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UCLASS(): Marks a class for reflection, enabling the creation of a corresponding UClass object."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UPROPERTY(): Exposes member variables to the reflection system, allowing GC tracking, Blueprint access, and saving/loading."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"UFUNCTION(): Exposes C++ functions to Blueprints and the reflection registry."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"#include "CoreMinimal.h"
#include "UObject/NoExportTypes.h"
#include "MyManagedObject.generated.h"

UCLASS(Blueprintable)
class UMyManagedObject : public UObject
{
    GENERATED_BODY()

public:
    UPROPERTY(BlueprintReadWrite, Category = "Data")
    FString ObjectName;

    UPROPERTY()
    class UMyManagedObject* ChildReference;
};"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Garbage Collection Mechanics"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike standard C++ where memory management is manual, Unreal Engine uses an automatic Garbage Collector for all UObject-derived instances. The GC operates on a reference-tracking, mark-and-sweep algorithm. It periodically scans the object graph starting from a set of known roots."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Root Set: Objects permanently referenced by the engine (like World, GameInstance, or objects explicitly marked via AddToRoot()). These are never collected."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reachability Analysis: The GC traverses all UPROPERTY pointers from root objects. Any UObject not reached during this traversal is flagged as unreachable."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sweeping: During the purge phase, unreachable objects have their destructors called, and their memory is reclaimed."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Avoiding Dangling Pointers with UPROPERTY"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common pitfall for developers coming from standard C++ is using raw pointers for UObjects without UPROPERTY macro decoration. If the GC destroys an object, raw pointers do not automatically null out, leading to dangling pointers and crashes."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// INCORRECT: GC is unaware of this pointer. If MyObj is destroyed, this becomes a dangling pointer.
UMyManagedObject* UnsafePointer;

// CORRECT: GC tracks this reference, prevents premature collection, and automatically nulls out on destruction.
UPROPERTY()
UMyManagedObject* SafePointer;"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Memory Management"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always decorate member UObject pointers with UPROPERTY() unless managing them through explicit container structures designed for non-UObject types."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not manually call delete on a UObject. Use MarkPendingKill() (historically) or let the GC sweep it naturally by removing references."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be cautious with circular references (e.g., Object A points to Object B, and Object B points to Object A via UPROPERTY). While GC handles cycles fine, they will never be collected unless both become unreachable from the Root Set."}]
